---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PlaceSection from "../components/PlaceSection.astro";
import PlaceVisual from "../components/PlaceVisual.astro";
import TimeSlider from "../components/TimeSlider.astro";
import { PLACES } from "../data/places";
import { SOURCES } from "../data/sources";
import { getStateForPlace } from "../lib/interaction";
import { assetUrl } from "../lib/asset-url";
---

<Layout title="When Humans Vanish">
  <header class="site-header">
    <nav aria-label="Primary" class="site-header-nav">
      <a href="./" class="site-header-brand">When Humans Vanish</a>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main>
    {/*
      The words come first in the source and sit second on screen. Reading
      order should open on the h1, but the eye should meet the planet first,
      and CSS grid can disagree with the DOM about that without either being
      wrong. On a phone the two agree again: heading, then text, then the
      globe underneath.
    */}
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>When Humans Vanish</h1>
        <p data-testid="intro" class="hero-intro">
          Human absence doesn't land on every place equally. The more a place's
          condition depends on work that never stops, the faster it responds
          once that work does. Scrub each place's own timeline to see how —
          then put any of them side by side at the same moment, and look at
          where five hundred years leaves all four.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/*
        The same planet that closes the page, at the size it starts from —
        one still frame of it, built out of the same two layers so the two
        ends of the page are recognisably the same object rather than two
        different pictures of an Earth.
      */}
      <div class="hero-globe" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="hero-globe-body" data-testid="hero-globe">
          <div
            class="hero-globe-map"
            style={`background-image: url(${assetUrl("globe/summer.jpg")})`}
          >
          </div>
          <div class="globe-shade"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {PLACES.map((place) => <PlaceSection place={place} />)}

    <section class="compare-section" data-testid="compare-section">
      <h2>Compare places</h2>
      <p>
        Choose any subset of places and move through the same span of time
        together.
      </p>
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Compare places</legend>
        {PLACES.map((place) => (
          <label>
            <input type="checkbox" data-testid="compare-toggle" data-place-id={place.id} checked />
            {place.name}
          </label>
        ))}
      </fieldset>
      <div class="compare-grid" data-testid="compare-grid">
        {PLACES.map((place) => (
          <PlaceVisual place={place} scope="compare" stop={getStateForPlace(place, 0)} />
        ))}
      </div>
      {/*
        Two plain buttons rather than one that changes meaning under the
        cursor. Both stay enabled and both are safe to press twice: pressing
        Play while it is already running does nothing, and so does Pause while
        it is already stopped. `data-playing` is the one piece of state, set
        here so the built page says plainly that nothing autoplays on load.
      */}
      <div
        class="compare-section-controls"
        data-testid="compare-controls"
        data-playing="false"
      >
        <TimeSlider scope="compare" />
        {/*
          Icon-only, so the label moves to aria-label — without it these read as
          just "button" to a screen reader. The glyphs themselves are marked
          aria-hidden so they add nothing to that name.

          The triangle's points are set so its centroid lands on x=12 rather
          than its bounding box, because a triangle centred by its box looks
          left-of-centre inside a circle.
        */}
        <div class="compare-section-transport">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="transport-button"
            data-testid="compare-play"
            aria-label="Play the timeline"
          >
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <path d="M8.5 5.5 L19 12 L8.5 18.5 Z"></path>
            </svg>
          </button>
          <button
            type="button"
            class="transport-button"
            data-testid="compare-pause"
            aria-label="Pause the timeline"
          >
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <rect x="7.5" y="5.5" width="3.5" height="13" rx="1"></rect>
              <rect x="13" y="5.5" width="3.5" height="13" rx="1"></rect>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  {/*
    The epilogue. It sits outside <main> so it can run edge to edge without
    fighting main's max-width — the usual full-bleed trick (width: 100vw plus
    negative margins) overflows by the width of the scrollbar, which would show
    up as horizontal scroll on the page.

    The four seasons are not a tint effect: they are four real NASA composites
    of the same year, so the snow line and the vegetation actually move. The
    layers are CSS backgrounds rather than <img>, because they are decoration
    with no informational content of their own and inventing alt text for them
    would only add noise for a screen reader — the words below are the content.
  */}
  <section class="globe-section" data-testid="globe-section">
    {/*
      The lead-in. Purely a change of sky, so it carries no content and is
      hidden from assistive tech entirely — there is nothing here to announce,
      and the words below are unaffected by it.
    */}
    <div class="globe-approach" data-testid="globe-approach" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="globe-stars" data-fade="in"></div>
    </div>

    {/*
      The track is what the stage is pinned against: its height is the length
      of the pinned run, and nothing else. Keeping it separate from the section
      is what lets the tail below exist at all — with the stage sticky directly
      inside the section, anything placed after it would have been overlapped
      by the still-pinned stage instead of following it.
    */}
    <div class="globe-track" data-testid="globe-track">
      <div class="globe-stage">
        <div class="globe-stars" aria-hidden="true"></div>

        <div class="globe" data-testid="globe" aria-hidden="true">
          {(["winter", "spring", "summer", "autumn"] as const).map((season) => (
            <div
              class="globe-map"
              data-season={season}
              style={`background-image: url(${assetUrl(`globe/${season}.jpg`)})`}
            />
          ))}
          <div class="globe-shade"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="globe-copy" data-testid="closing-question">
          <h2>A question, not a conclusion</h2>
          <p>
            In 4.5 billion years, countless species have appeared on this
            planet and countless have gone. What do we — everything we make,
            everything we live for — actually mean to something like Earth?
          </p>
          <p>
            Four places that depend on us to completely different degrees, run
            forward five hundred years, and arrive at much the same place. Held
            against 4.5 billion years, maybe the honest response is to put our
            arrogance away.
          </p>
          <p class="globe-copy-invite">
            If it leaves you with something different, I'd like to hear it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/*
      Open sky after the words. Without it the stage unpins straight onto the
      footer, and the page goes from deep space to a teal bar in one scroll —
      the same abruptness the approach above was added to fix, just at the
      other end.
    */}
    <div class="globe-tail" data-testid="globe-tail" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="globe-stars"></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <footer class="site-footer">
    <div class="site-footer-inner">
      <p class="site-footer-note">
        Every stop above is grounded in a real source or marked as a reasoned
        inference, never presented as more certain than it is.
      </p>
      <h2 class="site-footer-heading">Sources</h2>
      <ul class="site-footer-sources">
        {Object.values(SOURCES).map((source) => (
          <li><a href={source.url}>{source.title}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="site-footer-credit">
        Globe imagery: four monthly composites from{" "}
        <a href="https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/features/BlueMarble">
          Blue Marble: Next Generation
        </a>{" "}
        — NASA Earth Observatory (Reto Stöckli). Public domain.
      </p>
    </div>
  </footer>
  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
  <script src="../scripts/globe.ts"></script>
</Layout>
